{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794600364,"nonce":"tGozMxJ2cbixOKivb+AKNCse10o=","message":"Landed in 1f93780216200b3a39053de900593f0922d378d8. Corrections and results.\n\nCorrection to the description: the \"20 to 44 frames a second while covered, against 55 to 60 before and after\" was the example's own 2 s reorder cycle, not occlusion. The second copy at the default position did not cover the first in later runs (no Occluded(true) arrived). A reliable cover is a maximized second window (flip_window_check now takes POS and MAX).\n\nMeasured with a real occlusion (the first window got Occluded(true), the cover was open for 14 s), flip_window_check:\n- Before: the covered window kept drawing, about 30 frames a second (20 and 40 alternating with the reorder cycle), with 120 loop callbacks a second.\n- After: 0 callbacks and 0 drawn frames for the whole 13 s. On Occluded(false) it requested a redraw and the first frames after cost 0.6 to 0.7 ms, drawn 100 ms after the uncover.\n\nmacOS only (cfg!(target_os = \"macos\")): Linux, Windows and the others keep presenting. The Wayland swapchain starves without presents, so Occluded is not acted on there.\n\nNot done, and not claimed: this does not remove the stalls at the focus and occlusion transitions (the in-flight frame can still block), nor the 0.3 to 1 s ones, which never reproduced on demand. Not checked: a minimized window and another Space report Occluded the same way in practice (winit documents it), screen capture of a covered window (it stops updating).","files":null}]}